+      "literal_comparison_clauses": [
+        {
+          "section": "5",
+          "text": "# 5. Conceptual Principles\n\n## 5.1. AP-001 — Documentation as Knowledge\n\nDocumentation shall represent organized knowledge through logical entities, identified sources and applicable contracts. A file is a representation, not automatically a registered capability or a complete knowledge system. Normalized sources and functional files contribute within their respective scopes; DF defines documentary requirements while an applicable process produces/manages representations.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Assess purpose, actual source organization, relationships and coverage. The principle creates neither a document component nor proof that every file contributes complete or consistent knowledge.\n\n---\n\n## 5.2. AP-002 — Explicit Architecture\n\nArchitectural decisions shall identify the subject, scope, responsible owners, assumptions, applicable contracts and rationale explicitly. Significant allocation, classification, identity or required-contract changes require previous/new mapping and affected editions before propagation. Record unresolved decisions or missing evidence rather than treating an implicit label, arrow or directory as authority.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Examine actual decision/owner/reference records and limits. Explicit documentation proves neither approval nor complete implementation; derived summaries preserve authoritative provenance.\n\n---\n\n## 5.3. AP-003 — Technology Independence\n\nArchitectural definitions shall avoid prescribing technology unless an explicit integration constraint requires it. Logical identity, classification and domain meaning are distinct from filenames, formats, repositories and storage technology. Actual implementations still require representations, adapters, interfaces and executors under their applicable contracts.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Assess declared technology constraints and actual representation/adapter compatibility. Conceptual independence does not guarantee every format/tool is suitable, an implementation exists or technologies can evolve without consumer impact.\n\n---\n\n## 5.4. AP-004 — Single Source of Truth\n\nEach semantic concept or authoritative record shall have one identified responsible owner within its declared domain/scope. Metadata Dictionary defines descriptor meaning while Identity, Classification and Governance retain their domain semantics. Traceable summaries, catalogs and projections may repeat derived values with source-edition provenance and refresh/state limits without acquiring competing authority.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Check responsible definition/record sources, derived-copy provenance and unresolved conflicts. Logical single authority does not require one physical copy, a universal Registry route or a complete canonical catalog merely from this principle.\n\n---\n\n## 5.5. AP-005 — Semantic Consistency\n\nConsuming representations shall preserve applicable owner meanings and identify subjects/qualified contexts. Controlled aliases and historic vocabulary retain explicit mappings; unqualified overloads or competing meanings are recorded and reconciled. Metadata represents domain values, Schema organizes references, Profile constrains usage and Instance provides values under one effective Profile without redefining semantics.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Assess actual definitions, profiles, represented values and consumer scope. This principle does not manufacture missing Element/Profile records or prove consistency of unseen sources.\n\n---\n\n"
+        },
+        {
+          "section": "6",
+          "text": "# 6. Structural Principles\n\n## 6.1. AP-006 — Separation of Responsibilities\n\nEach registered capability shall have a defined primary semantic responsibility and primary allocation. Explicit collaboration, cross-cutting participation, shared check domains and traceable projections are permitted while preserving owner boundaries. Reviews may use Validation/Consistency Verification results without acquiring their rule ownership or approval authority.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Check primary owner/consumer contracts and actual support interactions. Prevent competing semantics rather than prohibiting every overlap of supporting work; no new capability or reassignment is inferred from a concern or directory.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2. AP-007 — Layered Architecture\n\nStructural allocation shall follow Architectural Layers:seven areas are Foundation, Architecture, Documentation, Governance, Discovery, Automation and Integration. Foundation/Architecture supply supporting definitions; the supplied 23 capabilities retain their 4/4/5/5/5 allocation. Five conceptual concerns—Knowledge, Documentation, Governance, Discovery and Preservation—are a different view.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** For required cross-Layer consumption, record consumer→provider contract/scope/source editions and apply the declared support order:the provider precedes its consumer. Same-Layer requirements, including Knowledge Graph→Relationships and Workflow→Policies, need explicit interfaces/ownership and cycle assessment. There is no obligation to consume every earlier provider or an adjacent serial service chain. Genuine required cycles are prohibited in the governed scope and remain visible for reconciliation; no projection/reference label hides them. Changing constraints requires assessed architectural revision, not an arbitrary upward exception. These rules certify neither a full graph nor implemented layer services.\n\n---\n\n## 6.3. AP-008 — Modular Design\n\nComponents shall preserve coherent boundaries and compatible provider contracts where feasible. Separate areas or documents may still require coordinated content/descriptor, owner/consumer or release changes. Independent evolution is an assessed objective under actual dependencies, not an unconditional guarantee.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Map affected exact editions, provider compatibility and transition evidence. A new component or module label alone proves neither independent release nor operational modularity.\n\n---\n\n## 6.4. AP-009 — Explicit Relationships\n\nApplicable connections shall be explicitly typed with identified endpoints and provenance. Distinguish documentary authority references, semantic associations, representation/projection, navigation, required consumption and execution. A required edge names consumer, provider, contract, scope and responsible source editions; physical directories, names and visual order alone establish no edge.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Check actual connection records and resolvable endpoints. Preserve genuine requirements/cycles visibly and reconcile conflicting owner contracts; do not rename an indispensable provider as navigation to create apparent acyclicity. This principle imposes no nonempty relationship list on every file.\n\n---\n\n## 6.5. AP-010 — Stable Identity\n\nNormalized documents shall preserve valid permanent logical identity under the applicable Domain+Identifier allocation/preservation contract. Identifier representation, current classification, title, Version/Revision, Status and physical location are distinct facts. Existing valid DF-SSS-NNN allocations do not automatically change with Family/Type or path; necessary migration needs a mapped assessed identity change.\n\n**Evidence boundary:** Apply the functional-file exclusion in §3 first:README.md, CHANGELOG.md and ArchitecturalMilestones.md require no normalized ID by this principle. Assess actual uniqueness/resolution and collision evidence; no 000 functional identity, missing allocation or universal generation algorithm is invented.\n\n---\n\n"
+        }
+      ],
